      "source": "News On AIR",
      "title": "9 mins ago | National 6 150 years of Vande Mataram will be major highlight of 80th ID celebrations: Defence Secretary The 80th Independence Day celebrations this year will highlight Yuva Shakti and the contribution of young India towards building a Viksit Bharat by 2047, along with the commemoration of 150 years of the National Song, Vande Mataram. Briefing media in New Delhi today, Defence Secretary Rajesh Kumar Singh said, the celebrations will be led by Prime Minister Narendra Modi from the historic Red Fort. The Prime Minister will unfurl the National Flag and address the nation. A major highlight of...",
      "url": "https://newsonair.gov.in/150-years-of-vande-mataram-will-be-major-highlight-of-80th-id-celebrations-defence-secretary/",
      "date": "Mon, 10 Aug 2026 18:50:57 IST",
      "content": "The 80th Independence Day celebrations this year will highlight Yuva Shakti and the contribution of young India towards building a Viksit Bharat by 2047, along with the commemoration of 150 years of the National Song, Vande Mataram. Briefing media in New Delhi today, Defence Secretary Rajesh Kumar Singh said, the celebrations will be led by Prime Minister Narendra Modi from the historic Red Fort. The Prime Minister will unfurl the National Flag and address the nation. A major highlight of this year’s celebrations will be the commemoration of 150 years of Vande Mataram. For the first time, the National Song will be sung from the ramparts of the Red Fort during the Independence Day ceremony. The ramparts will be decorated with floral arrangements based on the theme of Vande Mataram, while around 2 thousand 5 hundred NCC cadets and My Bharat volunteers will form a ‘Vande Mataram’ formation on Gyanpath. Vande Mataram will also be sung collectively at the Red Fort with Service bands, with everyone present standing at attention. Following the Prime Minister’s address, NCC cadets and My Bharat volunteers will sing the National Song, followed by the National Anthem. Mi-17 helicopters will participate in the ceremony, with the aerial display featuring the National Flag, flower petals and a special banner commemorating 150 years of Vande Mataram. As part of the nationwide celebrations, armed forces and other uniformed service bands will perform at 343 prominent locations from 8th to 15th of this month. Bands of the Army, Navy, Air Force, Coast Guard, NCC, CRPF, ITBP, CISF, SSB, BSF, IDS, RPF and Assam Rifles will participate. The second major theme of the celebration is ‘Yuva Shakti – Leading the Journey to Viksit Bharat@2047’. Nineteen medal-winning students from the 2026 International Physics, Chemistry, Biology and Mathematics Olympiads have been invited as special guests, along with young innovators, PM Internship Scheme interns, startups, PM Kaushal Vikas Yojana trainees, My Bharat volunteers and beneficiaries of various government schemes. Around 5 hundred Delhi government school students, selected through quizzes and competitions, will also attend the ceremony. The celebrations will also recognize other contributors to the Viksit Bharat vision, including women entrepreneurs supported under PM Mudra, PMAY-U beneficiaries, innovators and scientists, Bharat Taxi Sarthis, PM Surya Ghar beneficiaries, PM Vishwakarma artisans, PM Shram Yogi Maandhan beneficiaries, farmers and International Yoga Day volunteers. More than 15 hundred Delhi-based people representing different States and Union Territories and dressed in traditional attire have been invited. For invitees, Delhi Metro services will begin at 4 AM and will be free of cost. Around 2 hundred My Bharat and NCC volunteers will assist visitors, including those requiring wheelchair assistance. Cloakrooms, drinking water, toilets and rain ponchos will also be available, while parking locations will be provided through Google Maps and Mappls. After the ceremony, NCC cadets and My Bharat volunteers will undertake a Swachhata campaign at the Red Fort, focusing on systematic plogging and prompt removal of garbage.",
      "content_chars": 3210,
      "content_words": 488,
      "type": "Article"
    },
    {
      "source": "News On AIR",
      "title": "2 hours ago | National 107 Parliament passes Bankers’ Books Evidence Bill to recognise digital banking records as lega...",
      "url": "https://newsonair.gov.in/parliament-passes-bankers-books-evidence-bill-to-recognise-digital-banking-records-as-legal-evidence/",
      "date": "Mon, 10 Aug 2026 16:50:59 IST",
      "content": "Parliament has passed the Bankers’ Books Evidence Bill, 2026, with the Rajya Sabha approving it today. Lok Sabha has already passed the Bill. Union Finance Minister Nirmala Sitharaman moved the Bill, which aims to modernise the legal framework to meet the requirements of the present banking system. The Bill seeks to provide a law relating to evidence with respect to bankers’ books and to align it with contemporary digital banking practices. The Bill also has a provision that an electronic or digital record of a banker’s book will be admissible, valid, and legally enforceable as evidence. Replying to discussion on the bill, Finance Minister Sitharaman said that this bill is a very reformative step, which will help the country move forward at a faster pace. She highlighted that the Bankers’ Books Evidence Act was framed in 1891, when banking systems were entirely paper-based. Mrs Sitharaman said, today, India has demonstrated remarkable progress in digitising its economy. She said, banking and financial transactions are now largely driven by digital platforms, often accessible through a simple click on a mobile phone. The Minister said that in such a scenario, it is imperative that the country’s legal framework evolves to keep pace with these advancements. She noted that the existing 1891 Act relies heavily on certified copies of physical bank records as admissible evidence in legal proceedings. Mr Sitharaman said that the Bill establishes a technology-neutral legal framework for bankers’ records, which formally recognises electronic and digital banking records and ensures their admissibility in legal proceedings. It also standardises the certification process and allows authentication through manual, digital, and electronic signatures. Earlier, participating in the discussion, Arun Singh of BJP said that new legislation has been brought to change old act framed in 1891 under British rule. Mr Singh said, this bill will further boost the banking system, which is considered as the backbone of the Indian economy. He said, a revolutionary transformation is being witnessed in the banking system under the leadership of Prime Minister Narendra Modi. Mr Singh said, the bill has the provision that evidences in the digital or computerised form will be accepted as part of evidence. Manas Ranjan Mangaraj of BJD supported the objectives of the bill. He said, data security and privacy must be given the highest priority. He said, as the banking records contain sensitive personal and financial information, the government must ensure strong safeguards to prevent misuse, unauthorized access and cyber threats. Other members also spoke on the bill",
      "content_chars": 2673,
      "content_words": 414,
      "type": "Article"
    },
    {
      "source": "News On AIR",
      "title": "3 hours ago | National 179 LS passes Tribunals Reforms Bill, aims to improve transparency & efficiency of Tribunals",
      "url": "https://newsonair.gov.in/parliament-to-take-up-several-key-bills/",
      "date": "Mon, 10 Aug 2026 15:51:03 IST",
      "content": "Lok Sabha has passed the Tribunals Reforms Bill, 2026. The Bill was passed without discussion amidst opposition uproar. As the pandemonium continued, the Chair adjourned the House for the day to meet again at 11:00 AM tomorrow. Earlier, when the House reassembled after the second adjournment at 2:00 PM, Minister of State for Law and Justice Arjun Ram Meghwal moved the Tribunals Reforms Bill, 2026, amid the opposition uproar. The bill aims to improve efficiency, ensure independence, transparency, and uniformity in the qualifications, appointment of service of Chairpersons and Members of various Tribunals, as well as the administration and functioning of the Tribunals. The legislation also proposes to establish a National Tribunals Commission. Mr Meghwal said that the Bill has been brought as part of the reform express being rolled out across various sectors under the leadership of Prime Minister Narendra Modi. He said, the purpose of tribunals is not to replace courts but to function as complementary institutions in modern governance. They deal with specialized matters such as taxation, company law, environment, securities market, intellectual property, and other regulatory domains, with the objective of ensuring speedy and effective delivery of justice. Mr Meghwal highlighted that under the leadership of Mr Modi, the government initiated the process of rationalisation of tribunals in 2015. The Minister said that the Tribunal Reforms Bill, 2026 has been brought to establish a modern, independent, and uniform tribunal ecosystem. Mr Meghwal highlighted that the bill is aimed at strengthening governance, appointments, institutional independence, transparency, and efficiency. He said, the National Tribunals Commission will be established and it will be chaired by a former Judge of the Supreme Court or a former Chief Justice of a High Court. . The Presiding Officer, Jagdambika Pal repeatedly requested the protesting members to back to their seat and allow the House to function. He said that the entire nation is watching that who is responsible for the disruption of the House. Mr Pal said, the House is meant for discussion, but since the very first day of the Monsoon Session, the opposition members are continuously causing disruptions. Mr Pal said that Parliamentary Affairs Minister Kiren Rijiju has already accepted their demand that Union Home Minister Amit Shah is ready to give a statement on the issue related to the student protest.",
